ich had looked so huge when  we were up close to it suddenly looked  completely different in terms of  something it was tiny has as he set out  on this on this flight you can see in  the background there the big ferries  that go every night to and from from  Athens and you can see the inflatables  that are accompanying it in case the  aircraft had to had to ditch we were  prepared to take the airplane under tow  we had tried to absorb all of the  lessons from the Albatross project and  albatrosses strategy for example was  that I had to take a fishing line and  try to hook a ring on the aircraft  Daedalus had I had a refine system that  had the tow line in the airplanes on the  event that Kanellis wanted to be taken  under tow he would have dropped the line  and and the the boat underneath taken it  up and towed it navigation was done from  a command boat following behind the the  aircraft there this is a nice shot  looking up you can see the see the  carbon structure there and the region of  laminar flow on the top it really is  laminar back to about 60% pretty much  you can see where the where the foam  foam goes to on the on the top surface  the bottom is almost all laminar it  didn't need the the foam and you get  another view this is looking straight  down on the aircraft and again a good  view of the laminar flow we had escorts  provided by the the Greek Navy and the  Greek Coast Guard they were there their  primary role was for in case we needed  any emergency medical rescue that was  provided by the by the boats and they  also helped keep the keep the route  clear from other from other boats that  would have gotten in the way this is a  great picture gives you a sense of the  speed at which Kanellis was was flying  and also the crosswinds that he was  managing you can see the the crab in  there the boats obviously and the wakes  are marking the the direct flight route  to Santorini there and and this picture  gives you a good sense of the smooth  surface of the upper upper part  the wing this is a another good example  of the doing more with less that Paul  was just talking about where the the  speedboat there has what is that is that  a 120 horse motor on the 130 horse motor  on the on the back of that speedboat  there the Daedalus has a quarter  horsepower motor in it and they're going  the same speed and it's all it's all due  to the refinement of the technology and  the application of technology to to to  energy efficiency here the the flight  had been projected to take as long as  six hours we train for six hours but the  conditions were good and and and  Kanellis was just fantastic his heart  rate when he started was about a hundred  and fifty six and declined throughout  the flight by the time we were inside of  santorini his heart rate was down to 126  which still his absence just boggles my  mind how cool he was and throughout all  of this we had actually by this time  most of the team had deployed to the  landing site and was was was watching  marking the the site there with with  smoke and then Kanellis had the choice  of going straight in onto the beach a  lot has been made of the fact that the  aircraft broke up we were I'm here to  say we were maneuvering this thing to  try to get it back in one piece can also  easily could have gone straight in onto  the beach if the objective was to was  just to get it onto the onto the beach  but we really wanted to try to maneuver  the airplane in and land it we had all  seen that Society of Brian Allen  slipping it in there under the beach in  France and that was pretty much the  maneuver we were working on here so  Kanellis came in at fairly high speed  turns in parallel to the beach and tries  to to slip it in parallel here you can  see the the beginning of the fail you  can actually see the buckling right  there in the in the carbon tail boom as  the as a gust of wind thermally induced  coming off this black sand catches this  torques the tail tail fails right there  the tail boom fails the wires are the  cables that control the elevator are  outside at that point says this torques  that puts a full command on the on the  horizontal stabbed there  / stresses the way it fails out and  right near a transport joint on the on  the right hand side and the aircraft  drops into the into the into the water  they are just off off the shore we had a  lot of people help us take it apart at  this point that was the problem there if  you go to Santorini I'm sure there's  still pink pink foam souvenirs in some  of the shops there and and there's  Kanellis after the after the flight  whoo-hoo no kidding could have gone on  for for quite a while on this flight  and that's the team there on the on the  beach many of whom are here today could  I get everybody who is on the team to  stand up at this point everybody who  worked on the project could you stand  many of the people I think haven't seen  each other in 10 years after this and I  really do thank ed Crawley for pulling  this whole thing together  well the devilís project always you know  it was done for education it was done  for research it was a marvelous project  it could never answer the question of so  what and when people asked it in the  lectures but but one of the interesting  things about science is that it really  when you really are doing research is  that that you never know where the the  next problem is going to come from in it  and in Daedalus for me anyway it came  from a very unexpected source in in the  mid-80s there were two things that  emerged that kind of shifted the  paradigm from nuclear holocaust being  the sort of you know big picture issue  that that that kind of hung over all of  us and those two issues were the  emergence of AIDS the break out of that  into the general population and the  depletion of stratospheric ozone in  terms of issues that when they first  began to appear no one was quite sure  how bad this was going to be and that  clearly had the potential to to to  eliminate life as as we knew it if in a  worst-case scenario the the ozone hole  obviously appeared over Antarctica in  the in the early 80s and in the mid 80s  a lot of attention was directed towards  figuring out exactly what it was the  primary tools for doing that were large  stratospheric balloons and the an  aircraft called the ER to which NASA  operates it's a version of the military  spy plane and these worked okay for  figuring out the for the causes of the  Antarctic ozone hole and and providing  the conclusive links between  chlorofluorocarbons and the and the  catalytic cycles that destroyed the  ozone but it was also clear that the  scientists to answer the question of is  this going to spread to other places on  the planet is this going to happen in  the northern hemisphere is it going to  happen over the middle latitudes that  you needed to go out and take  measurements at higher altitudes and in  more places around the planet and a  couple of us from from the Daedalus  project linked up with a group down at  Harvard in the atmospheric research  project there and began construction of  a series of aircraft that would attempt  to apply the aerodynamics and the  structures and some of the project  management philosophies that came out of  Daedalus and applied them to a series of  small very customized robotic aircraft  that could be used for atmospheric  science high altitude research initially  in the ozone area and we built an  aircraft just called Perseus about 60 62  feet in in wingspan developed the  controls technology for that and made a  series of flights this is really to give  you a sense of scale of the of the  Perseus this particular aircraft has  carried about a 220 pound payload to an  altitude of about 50,000 feet and and  both the solar plane and the the Perseus  aircraft are are working together our  joint participants in a NASA program  that's developing the technology for  future operational aircraft and we've  also 